 `usrsctp_recvv()` returns the number of bytes received, or -1 if an error occurred.  The variable errno is then set appropriately.
 
+## SNAP
+With SNAP, the SCTP Negotiation Acceleration Protocol ([draft-uberti-tsvwg-warp](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-uberti-tsvwg-warp/), section 4.1), both endpoints exchange their INIT chunks out-of-band, e.g. in the SDP, instead of running the SCTP handshake.
+It works on one-to-one `AF_CONN` sockets; on one-to-many sockets the functions fail with `EOPNOTSUPP`.
+
+### usrsctp_snap_prepare()
+
+```c
+int
+usrsctp_snap_prepare(struct socket *so,
+                     struct sockaddr *name,
+                     socklen_t namelen,
+                     void *init,
+                     size_t *init_len)
+```
+
+* so: Pointer to the socket as returned by `usrsctp_socket()`.
+* name: Address of the peer.
+* namelen: Size of the peer's address.
+* init: Buffer for the INIT chunk.
+* init_len: Size of `init`; on return, the number of bytes copied.
+
+`usrsctp_snap_prepare()` sets the association up like `usrsctp_connect()`, but copies its INIT chunk into `init` instead of sending it. Later calls copy the same chunk.
+
+`usrsctp_snap_prepare()` returns 0 on success and -1 in case of an error. With `ENOBUFS`, `*init_len` is the size needed.
+
 ## Socket Options
 Socket options are used to change the default behavior of socket calls.
 Their behavior is specified in [RFC 6458](https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc6458). The functions to get or set them are
diff --git a/usrsctplib/netinet/sctp_output.c b/usrsctplib/netinet/sctp_output.c
index 7a3381a..b82f3eb 100755
--- a/usrsctplib/netinet/sctp_output.c
+++ b/usrsctplib/netinet/sctp_output.c
@@ -5379,9 +5379,51 @@ sctp_send_initiate_internal(struct sctp_inpcb *inp, struct sctp_tcb *stcb, int s
 void
 sctp_send_initiate(struct sctp_inpcb *inp, struct sctp_tcb *stcb, int so_locked)
 {
+#if defined(__Userspace__)
+	/* SNAP: an INIT on the wire ends the out-of-band exchange. */
+	stcb->asoc.snap_prepared = 0;
+#endif
 	sctp_send_initiate_internal(inp, stcb, so_locked, NULL);
 }
 
+#if defined(__Userspace__)
+/*
+ * SNAP: copies the association's INIT chunk into buf, building it on the
+ * first call, without sending it. On ENOBUFS *len is the size needed.
+ */
+int
+sctp_snap_copy_initiate(struct sctp_inpcb *inp, struct sctp_tcb *stcb, void *buf, size_t *len)
+{
+	struct mbuf *m = NULL;
+	uint32_t init_len;
+
+	if (stcb->asoc.snap_init == NULL) {
+		sctp_send_initiate_internal(inp, stcb, SCTP_SO_NOT_LOCKED, &m);
+		if (m == NULL) {
+			return (ENOMEM);
+		}
+		init_len = sctp_calculate_len(m);
+		/* Keep only the chunk, not the cluster it was built in. */
+		stcb->asoc.snap_init = sctp_get_mbuf_for_msg(init_len, 0, M_NOWAIT, 1, MT_DATA);
+		if (stcb->asoc.snap_init == NULL) {
+			sctp_m_freem(m);
+			return (ENOMEM);
+		}
+		m_copydata(m, 0, (int)init_len, mtod(stcb->asoc.snap_init, caddr_t));
+		SCTP_BUF_LEN(stcb->asoc.snap_init) = (int)init_len;
+		sctp_m_freem(m);
+	}
+	init_len = (uint32_t)SCTP_BUF_LEN(stcb->asoc.snap_init);
+	if (*len < init_len) {
+		*len = init_len;
+		return (ENOBUFS);
+	}
+	memcpy(buf, mtod(stcb->asoc.snap_init, caddr_t), init_len);
+	*len = init_len;
+	return (0);
+}
+#endif
+
